# Hand-dropped PDFs used for manual smoke/stress runs against the upload path.
# ~3 MB of binaries with no code referencing them; they are inputs a human picks
# by hand, not fixtures, so they stay local rather than in the repo.
scripts/testing_pdfs/

# Eval harness — results are local only; corpora/gold/config are committed
# NOTE: `results/*` not `results/` -- git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT
# DIRECTORY is excluded, so the negations below only work against a glob.
scripts/eval/results/*
scripts/eval/cache/
# ...but keep the append-only eval history. results/ is ignored because it holds
# large per-run exports, and that ignore is why this project has no before/after
# record of its own eval scores: run_eval.py overwrote scoreboard.json in place
# AND the directory was untracked, so nothing survived. The history files are
# small, append-only, and are the durable record.
!scripts/eval/results/history.jsonl
!scripts/eval/results/openreview_history.jsonl
# node_eval.jsonl is tracked too: ~16 KB for 14 records, and it is the ONLY
# per-node cost/latency record that survives a clone. Its records are bounded
# by (nodes x papers x repeat) per run, not by document count, so it does not
# run away the way the two sinks below do.
!scripts/eval/results/node_eval.jsonl
#
# Deliberately NOT tracked, and why:
#
#   results/node_eval_spans.jsonl  -- raw OTel-shaped spans, one line per span,
#     unbounded: every replay of every node appends. Nothing here is lost by
#     ignoring it; benchmarks.py distils the per-node latency and cost out of
#     it, and node_eval.jsonl (tracked, above) carries the same costs already.
#
#   cache/ingest_manifest.jsonl    -- 155 KB and grows by one record per
#     document per ingest run. Covered by the `scripts/eval/cache/` rule above.
#     BENCHMARKS.md carries the rollup that matters: extractor, chunking config,
#     embedding model+dims, token/chunk totals, error and ceiling counts.
#
#   results/retrieval_eval.jsonl   -- bimodal: a summary record is ~2.7 KB, but
#     a record carrying its per-query `misses` list is 66-99 KB, i.e. ~97% of
#     the file is one debug payload that is regenerable from the labels. The
#     distilled board carries EVERYTHING ELSE from these records -- every
#     metric with its n, config hash, both fingerprints, the recall ceiling and
#     percent-of-attainable, the validity flag and degradation reason, the
#     failure_breakdown (which is exactly the rollup of `misses`), the
#     retrieval_health counters and the corpus/resolution summary. Losing the
#     per-query miss list costs debugging convenience, not a benchmark.
#
# The distilled outputs are the durable record and ARE tracked. They live in
# scripts/eval/ (not results/), so no negation is needed for them -- listed
# here only so the intent is written down next to the exclusions:
#   scripts/eval/BENCHMARKS.md
#   scripts/eval/benchmarks.json
scripts/eval/pdfs/*.pdf
scripts/eval/corpora/**/*.pdf
scripts/eval/openreview/**/*.pdf
